EmbeddedChunk structure :

EmbeddedChunk
│
├── chunk
│     ├── chunk_id = tcp-001
│     ├── content = "TCP is ..."
│     ├── source_document = ...
│     └── section = ...
│
├── embedding
│     ├── -0.143...
│     ├──  0.872...
│     ├── ...
│     └── 384 values
│
└── embedding_model
      └── all-MiniLM-L6-v2
==================================================================================================================
Execution flow  of query(Why does HTTP use TCP ) in rag_demo.py  and retrievel :

Why did this happen?

Let's follow what happened internally.

Step 1

The query

Why does HTTP use TCP?

was converted into a 384-dimensional vector.

Conceptually

Question

↓

[-0.14,
 0.28,
 ...
 384 values]
Step 2

Chroma compared that vector against four stored vectors.

HTTP Vector

TCP Vector

UDP Vector

DNS Vector
Step 3

Instead of comparing words...

it compares meaning.

Conceptually

Query

↓

Similarity

↓

HTTP      0.91

TCP       0.87

UDP       0.42

DNS       0.31

( the scores are hypothetical, but this is the idea.)

Step 4

Top 2 are returned.

Exactly what happened.

HTTP

↓

TCP



Notice something amazing.

The query

Why does HTTP use TCP?

does not literally appear anywhere in your document.

There is no sentence saying

"HTTP uses TCP because..."

The embedding model understood the semantics.

That is the whole point of vector search.

Imagine keyword search

Suppose we used grep.

grep "Why does HTTP use TCP"

Result

Nothing

Yet semantic search finds

HTTP

TCP

because it understands that these chunks answer the question.
